{
  "id": "ep119",
  "duration": 53.525,
  "beats": [
    {
      "start": 0.08,
      "end": 4.281,
      "text": "A two-hundred-foot asteroid flew past Earth before anyone saw it.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Artist's Concept of Comet 238P-Read (2023-123).jpg",
        "credit": "ILLUSTRATION: NASA, ESA",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Artist%27s_Concept_of_Comet_238P-Read_(2023-123).jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 0.0,
          "end": 2.7,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 2.7,
          "end": 4.267,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 4.281,
      "end": 9.801,
      "text": "Most people believe space telescopes track every city-killing rock near our planet.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "The heart of the Milky Way (potw1830a).tiff",
        "credit": "Y. Beletsky (LCO)/ESO",
        "license": "CC BY 4.0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_heart_of_the_Milky_Way_(potw1830a).tiff"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 4.267,
          "end": 9.8,
          "kind": "cover",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 9.801,
      "end": 18.571,
      "text": "But on July 13, 2023, asteroid 2023 NT1 swept past at one-quarter of the distance to the Moon.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Moon Phase and Libration, 2026 South Up (SVS5588 - orbit).jpg",
        "credit": "NASA's Scientific Visualization Studio - USRA/Ernie Wright, NASA/GSFC/Noah Petro, eMITS/James Tralie",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Moon_Phase_and_Libration,_2026_South_Up_(SVS5588_-_orbit).jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 9.8,
          "end": 18.567,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 18.571,
      "end": 23.966,
      "text": "Approaching directly out of the Sun's blinding glare, the rock stayed completely hidden.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Magnificent CME Erupts on the Sun - August 31.jpg",
        "credit": "NASA Goddard Space Flight Center",
        "license": "CC BY 2.0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Magnificent_CME_Erupts_on_the_Sun_-_August_31.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 18.567,
          "end": 23.967,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 23.966,
      "end": 30.486,
      "text": "Two days after it passed, an ATLAS observatory telescope in South Africa finally spotted it.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "designed card"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 23.967,
          "end": 30.5,
          "kind": "designed",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 30.486,
      "end": 38.461,
      "text": "The Chelyabinsk meteor used this exact solar blind spot in 2013, injuring roughly fifteen hundred people.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "RM1886-Meteorit-chelyabinsk.jpg",
        "credit": "Joan Rosell, from rosellminerals.com",
        "license": "CC BY 4.0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:RM1886-Meteorit-chelyabinsk.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 30.5,
          "end": 38.467,
          "kind": "cover",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 38.461,
      "end": 47.481,
      "text": "To catch these blind-spot hazards, the European Space Agency is developing the NEOMIR spacecraft to launch around 2030.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Lagrange Points (29832072485).jpg",
        "credit": "NOAASatellites",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lagrange_Points_(29832072485).jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 38.467,
          "end": 43.4,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 43.4,
          "end": 47.467,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 47.481,
      "end": 53.175,
      "text": "Without satellites monitoring the Sun, solar glare can still blind our early warning systems.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Artist's Concept of Comet 238P-Read (2023-123).jpg",
        "credit": "ILLUSTRATION: NASA, ESA",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Artist%27s_Concept_of_Comet_238P-Read_(2023-123).jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 47.467,
          "end": 53.533,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
